Let $0 < a \le b \le c$. Since $T$ is a triangle, we have $a + b > c$.

If $T^{-1} = T(\frac{1}{a}, \frac{1}{b}, \frac{1}{c})$ is also a triangle, then $0 < \frac{1}{c} \le \frac{1}{b} \le \frac{1}{a}$, and $\frac{1}{b} + \frac{1}{c} > \frac{1}{a}$. Thus,
$$
\begin{cases}
a + b > c, \\
\frac{1}{b} + \frac{1}{c} > \frac{1}{a}
\end{cases}
\Rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\frac{a}{c} + \frac{b}{c} > 1, \\
\frac{c}{b} + 1 > \frac{c}{a}.
\end{cases}
$$

Let $x = \frac{a}{c}$, $y = \frac{b}{c}$. Then we need
$$
\begin{cases}
0 < x \le y \le 1, \\
x + y > 1, \\
\frac{1}{y} + 1 > \frac{1}{x}.
\end{cases}
$$

The third inequality is equivalent to $y < \frac{x}{1-x}$. Solving the boundary conditions
$$
\begin{cases}
x + y = 1, \\
\frac{1}{y} + 1 = \frac{1}{x}
\end{cases}
$$
yields $x^2 - 3x + 1 = 0$, giving $x = \frac{3 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}$. Since $0 < x < 1$, we take $x = \frac{3 - \sqrt{5}}{2}$.

Analysis shows that there exists a $y$ satisfying all constraints if and only if $x > \frac{3 - \sqrt{5}}{2}$. Therefore, if $T$ is invertible, then $x > \frac{3 - \sqrt{5}}{2}$, i.e., $a > \frac{3 - \sqrt{5}}{2}c$.

