We shall prove that for any positive number $x \neq 1$, $x \in A$ if and only if $\frac{1}{x} \in A$. Then, since $A$ is finite, we can pair up the elements of $A$ that are not equal to 1, yielding $\prod_{x \in A} x = 1$.

Take any real number $b \in (1, +\infty)$. Applying the condition with $a = b$ and $a = \frac{1}{b}$ respectively, we know that the sets $\{x \in A \mid x > b\}$ and $\{x \in A \mid x < \frac{1}{b}\}$ have the same parity of cardinality, and the sets $\{x \in A \mid x > \frac{1}{b}\}$ and $\{x \in A \mid x < b\}$ also have the same parity of cardinality. Therefore, the sets
$$
\begin{aligned}
L &= \{x \in A \mid x > \frac{1}{b}\} \setminus \{x \in A \mid x > b\} = \{x \in A \mid x \in (\frac{1}{b}, b]\}
\end{aligned}
$$
and
$$
\begin{aligned}
R &= \{x \in A \mid x < b\} \setminus \{x \in A \mid x < \frac{1}{b}\} = \{x \in A \mid x \in [\frac{1}{b}, b)\}
\end{aligned}
$$
have the same parity of cardinality.

But $L$ contains at most one element not in $R$, and vice versa. Hence, $L$ and $R$ have the same cardinality. Therefore, $b$ and $\frac{1}{b}$ either both belong to $A$ or both do not belong to $A$, and the conclusion is proved.

